WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.172
This practice is about explaining row multiplication, not just running the SQL.

00:00:05.172 --> 00:00:10.767
Focus on one patient who has several admissions, lab results, and prescriptions.

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:18.437
If those three child tables are all joined by patient_id, each admission can combine with each lab test and

00:00:18.437 --> 00:00:20.022
each prescription.

00:00:20.022 --> 00:00:25.497
That is why the number of joined rows can be much larger than the number of original events.

00:00:25.497 --> 00:00:29.512
A strong explanation should use the word combinations.

00:00:29.512 --> 00:00:33.737
The query is not creating new admissions or new lab tests.

00:00:33.737 --> 00:00:39.017
It is creating combinations of existing rows because several one-to-many relationships are being joined at the

00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:45.507
same time. Compare this with the pre-aggregation strategy from the earlier slide.

00:00:45.507 --> 00:00:52.107
When each child table is summarized to one row per patient first, the multiplication disappears.

00:00:52.107 --> 00:00:55.932
Do not memorize a rule such as always use DISTINCT.

00:00:55.932 --> 00:01:01.382
Instead, identify the target grain and the cardinality of every relationship.

00:01:01.382 --> 00:01:07.792
That reasoning is transferable to other projects such as customer analytics, educational analytics, finance,

00:01:07.792 --> 00:01:08.682
and operations.

00:01:08.682 --> 00:01:13.772
Whenever several detail tables connect to the same parent, row multiplication should be one of the first

00:01:13.772 --> 00:01:16.640
things you check.
